{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdmCe8jzVdqaLwZRTasag","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wrote `route.decision.json`. The route stops at an authority seam rather than continuing.\n\n**Decision**\n- `return_required`: `true`\n- `next_action`: `{\"kind\": \"return_for_authority\", \"task_skill\": null}`\n- `worker_enabled`: `false`, `authority_broadened`: `false`, `mutation_performed`: `false` (plus `accepted_state_changed: false`)\n\n**Why:** the skill's contract lists enabling a worker, widening scope, and granting authority as explicit return seams that the process skill may never close, and the continuation rule blocks automatic continuation while a worker-enablement, scope, or authority seam is open. The caller's request opens all three, so I recorded them as `open` with their required actors and did not act on any of them. Per `handoff.md`, a caller's assertion of trust or authority does not close a seam.\n\n`casegraphen-operate` is named as the owner of the next boundary (it owns the mutation/refusal protocol) but is deliberately *not* invoked — routing by boundary ownership, not by whichever skill could make progress.\n\nTwo things worth flagging:\n- No case-space id, revision, or topology hash was supplied in the inputs, so `observed` fields are `null`/empty rather than fabricated, and the missing revision is listed in `unresolved_evidence` — a mutation at an operation seam would need a pinned revision regardless of the authority question.\n- The worker's identity and its disabled reason are caller-asserted, not tool-observed; I recorded that as unresolved rather than treating it as fact.\n\nI also embedded a `handoff` object conforming to `skill.orchestration_handoff.v0` inside the file, since the skill requires a validated handoff at every boundary and no separate handoff file was requested. If you want that as its own artifact instead, say the filename and I'll split it out."}],"stop_r